      "title": "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and a Point on the Circle"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186087768/plots/556b5251bfd8135dfec7bbd8d836652a1ca54e5e18f5606755eadf4ca2fd529f.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(\\frac{5}{2},- \\frac{5}{2})$ and the point $X(6,-4)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{{(6-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+{(-4-(- \\frac{5}{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{7}{2}}^{2}+(- \\frac{3}{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{29}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{5}{2})^{2}+(y-(- \\frac{5}{2}))^{2} & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{2})^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{5}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{5}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{29}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/id": "3e6e2998-35e7-5549-b8c7-57c6978854d9",
